@* Table (UI docs). Sections: Usage, Examples, Customization, Styling reference, API, Accessibility, Guidelines.
   The "collection" example is used by the browser tests (BehaviourTests, DocsVisualTests): keep its markup and rows. *@

<ul class="dnf-docs-source">
    <li>CSS <code>src/DotNetForge.UI/Styles/components/table.css</code></li>
    <li>Behaviour <code>src/DotNetForge.UI/wwwroot/dnf.js</code> (collections: search, filters, sorting, paging, selection)</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="usage">Usage</h2>
<p>A table is a plain <code>&lt;table class="dnf-table__table"&gt;</code> inside a <code>dnf-table</code> wrapper that scrolls
    sideways when the columns don't fit. Put it straight into a <a href="@UiDocsCatalog.Root/components/card">card</a>, below
    the card header - the cells bring their own padding.</p>

<ui-doc-example id="basic" layout="block">
    <div class="dnf-card">
        <div class="dnf-card__header">
            <h3 class="dnf-card__title">Pages</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="dnf-table">
            <table class="dnf-table__table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">Title</th>
                        <th scope="col">Path</th>
                        <th scope="col">Updated</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Home</td><td><code>/</code></td><td>3 Oct 2026</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>About us</td><td><code>/about-us</code></td><td>28 Sep 2026</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Pricing</td><td><code>/pricing</code></td><td>12 Sep 2026</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </div>
</ui-doc-example>

<h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>

